WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660
Hey Nick! Can you do a video about Lagrange Points?

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.160
Sure! That sounds fun.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.720
Science Asylum is a proud partner with Dollar Shave Club.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:11.660
Hey Crazies.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.600
Extreme gravity like you’d find around black holes gets a lot of press,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.720
but you don’t have to take gravity to the extremes for it to do unexpected things.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.880
Sometimes even Newton’s laws are enough.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.420
Lagrange Points are one of those times.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.280
They’re five special points around the orbit of a planet or moon.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.260
Those Lagrange points orbit with the planet or moon,

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.780
so you can stick things in there like space probes and they’ll stay.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.340
It’s pretty cool actually.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.040
We take advantage of them all the time, at least the points around the Earth.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.040
There’s a whole list on Wikipedia.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.680
They love lists over there.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.720
But there’s kind of a glaring question here.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.140
Um, why do things stay there?

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.240
And, to answer that, we need to delve a little deeper into Newtonian gravity.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.780
The simplest example would be the Earth.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.300
If some tiny space rock wanders nearby, the Earth will exert a force on it,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.400
which, in turn, lets us predict the path it will take.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.760
That’s basically how Newtonian mechanics works.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.900
An unbalanced force causes an interesting motion.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.040
If there are no forces or all the forces are balanced, the path is just a boring straight line.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.980
Ok, so, the single force from the Earth can only do one of three things.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.920
It can speed the rock up, slow it down, or make it change direction,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.300
or any combination of those that makes sense.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.260
That’s what acceleration is.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.770
There’s nothing unusual about that.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.960
It’s typical of any unbalanced force.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.299
That’s pretty much what Newton’s second law says.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:37.060
To get the weird stuff like Lagrange points, we need two objects exerting a force simultaneously.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.340
Let’s consider the Earth and Moon together.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.180
This is what they look like to scale.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.460
Any tiny rock that drifts into this space is going to have a force on it from both.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:50.820
But, on the line connecting the Earth and Moon, the forces on the rock are in opposite directions.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.380
See where we’re going with this?

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.720
Somewhere on this line, the forces should cancel each other.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.720
Since the Moon is less massive than Earth, it should happen closer to the Moon.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.860
Right about here.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.960
Pretty cool, huh?

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.320
Except that’s not actually what we want.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.800
At first glance, that looks like Lagrange Point number 1, but it’s not.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.980
The forces on the rock might be balanced at the moment, but that won’t last very long

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.800
because the Earth and Moon are moving through space at different rates.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.250
That’s highly unstable.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.100
Lagrange points are always at least semi-stable.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.620
So where did we make a mistake?

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.320
We don’t actually want the force to be zero.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.500
Remember, these points move around as the Moon orbits.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.500
Anything at those points will be traveling along curved paths.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.140
Curved paths are accelerated paths

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.440
and acceleration means there should be an unbalanced force.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.620
So we need to be a little more careful.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.840
Rather than the two forces adding to zero in Newton’s second law,

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.060
we want them to add to give us curved motion instead.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.580
This is the centripetal or center seeking acceleration.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.860
Doing that puts the Lagrange point a bit farther from the Moon and a bit closer to the Earth.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.640
This is Lagrange point number 1, or L1 for short.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.380
It’s about 36,000 miles from the Moon

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.560
compared to the location from earlier which was only about 24,000 miles.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.380
You could use the same technique to find L2 and L3, but this isn’t very elegant.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:16.580
It requires us to already have some idea of where they are beforehand

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.500
and it isn’t very obvious why L4 and L5 even exist.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.540
There’s a better way to do this, but we need to do two things.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.920
First, we need a change in perspective.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.280
While we know the Moon orbits the Earth, this would be a lot easier if it didn’t,

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.040
so let’s pretend like it doesn’t.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.360
Can we just do that?

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.819
We do this kind of thing all the time.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.660
It’s called a coordinate transformation.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.739
Rather than having the Moon drag all the Lagrange points around,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:43.280
let’s imagine our coordinates are going around in the opposite direction.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.480
It’s like rotating our camera with the Moon’s orbit to make everything look stationary.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.880
The second thing we need to do is stop talking about forces all together.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.880
It’s going to be easier if we do this in terms of energy instead.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.580
That gives us something like this.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.860
We’ve got the gravitational energy from Earth, from the Moon,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.840
and from the rotation we’re not seeing.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.640
We should be able to map it across space and watch the Lagrange points just pop out.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.080
Here it is!

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.900
Hmm, that isn’t so helpful.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.520
Oh, I know! Let’s try shading instead of numbers.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:11.800
Boom!

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.740
OK, that’s not so helpful either.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.380
Maybe if we imagine the energy value as a height?

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:17.800
Oh.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.440
I see what the problem is.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.740
Lagrange points are actually really subtle, so we’re going to have to exaggerate things.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.980
But, you know, I think we can still make that last graphic work.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.440
Imagine you’re standing in the middle of a valley created by a circular mountain range.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.120
The first thing you’d see is this flat ledge.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.320
If you place a ball there, it’ll stay.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.800
Any deviation from side-to-side and the ball will simply roll back to the ledge.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.380
Any deviation forward or backward and the ball will roll away, down the cliff.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.140
That’s exactly how L1 works in space.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.280
Any deviation this direction and the space rock will return to L1.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.260
Any deviation the other way and the rock will fall toward the Earth or Moon.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:01.080
Mathematicians call that a saddle point and L2 and L3 work in a similar way.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.110
In the mountain analogy, you’ll find L2 on the slightly higher ledge behind L1.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.980
L3 is directly behind you, so you’ll need to pull a 180.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.320
Again, a deviation side-to-side and the ball will simply roll back.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.080
A deviation forward or backward and the ball will roll away.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.640
For that reason, these three points are considered semi-stable.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.320
L4 and L5 are a different story.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.580
You’ll find those on either side at the highest peaks.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.660
The mountains are really flat up there.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.900
They’re very stable locations.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.620
Wait wait, how can those be stable if the cliff goes down in all directions?

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.360
Yeah, well, that’s where our analogy breaks down.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.780
That’s the problem with analogies.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.520
They all break down somewhere.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.740
In space, any motion of the rock will activate the Coriolis Effect.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:50.340
That effect makes the path of the rock spiral back into L4 or L5.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.940
In our analogy, it’s as if the motion of the ball changes the mountain,

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.140
which sounds a little strange, but rotating frames are weird like that.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.159
Anyway, I’ve only been using the Moon’s orbit so I could draw things to scale.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.980
The most useful Lagrange points are actually on the Earth’s orbit around the Sun.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:11.200
If you want a space probe to monitor the Sun, you put it in L1, like SOHO.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.000
If you want it to always point away from the Sun, you put it in L2, like Planck.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.460
Unfortunately, those require course corrections every month or so.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:24.320
L3 lasts a bit longer, but it’s not much use since it’s always blocked by the Sun.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.360
It could never send us information.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.980
L4 and L5 are so stable, they tend to collect space junk naturally.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.000
They don’t even have to be related to the Earth.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.960
All orbit has these points.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.860
My favorites are L4 and L5 for Jupiter.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.360
They’re filled to the brim with asteroids.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.899
So, what are Lagrange Points?

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:45.600
They’re five special points around the orbit of a planet or moon.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.180
They orbit with the planet or moon, so they’re locations of stability.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.220
Objects can remain stationary with respect to the planet or moon.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.760
Some of them are fully stable, while others are only semi-stable,

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.400
but the two least stable points are actually the most useful.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.440
We regularly put space probes there for a consistent view of the universe.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.280
So, what do you think about Lagrange points?

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:07.520
Cool? Not cool?

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:08.900
Let us know in the comments.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.520
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.000
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

